<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="topic-4-6-ai-ethics">
  <time minutes="90" />
  <title>Ethics and Effects of AI</title>

  <idx>ai-ethics-effects</idx>
  <introduction>
    <p>Quick Links:
      <xref text="global" ref="ethical-design-evaluation, ai-society-economy-culture,
      LOs-4-6, vocab-4-6"/>
    </p>
    <p>
      In this topic, we evaluate AI systems with ethical design criteria and explain
      how AI impacts society, the economy, and culture.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="ethical-design-evaluation">
    <title>Evaluating AI with Ethical Design Criteria</title>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.A.1</title>
      <p>
        Ethical design uses criteria to evaluate AI systems, including fairness,
        transparency, privacy, safety, and accountability. Developers use these criteria
        to guide choices about data, model behavior, user experience, and safeguards.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.A.2</title>
      <p>
        Developers use evidence to evaluate whether a system is meeting ethical design
        criteria. Methods include testing performance across different groups and contexts,
        reviewing how data is collected and used, observing users to identify confusion or
        barriers, and examining how the system's outputs are used in decision making.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.A.3</title>
      <p>
        Ethical design often involves trade-offs, such as how personalization can conflict
        with privacy. Developers should document decisions, explain constraints, and choose
        designs that reduce risk and protect users.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.A.4</title>
      <p>
        Developers assess impacts before deployment and revise designs based on user feedback,
        then continue monitoring after release because real-world use can reveal new issues or
        model drift.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.A.5</title>
      <p>
        Documentation and testing support accountability by making it clear what the system does,
        what data it relies on, what limitations it has, and what safeguards or human review steps
        are in place when the risks are high.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.A.6</title>
      <p>
        To evaluate an AI system, people apply ethical design criteria by asking questions such as:
        Is the system fair across different users and contexts? Is it transparent about when it is
        used and what it is doing? Does it minimize data collection and protect privacy? Who is
        responsible when it fails? What protections reduce harm?
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.A.7</title>
      <p>
        Ethical evaluation includes proposing changes to reduce harm, such as improving disclosure,
        reducing data collection, adding human review for high-risk cases, expanding testing across
        groups, or giving users meaningful control like the ability to opt out or adjust settings.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="ai-society-economy-culture">
    <title>How AI Impacts Society, Economy, and Culture</title>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.B.1</title>
      <p>
        AI systems are designed to optimize specific goals, such as efficiency, engagement,
        accuracy, or generating options. Because the goal shapes what the system prioritizes,
        it helps explain the kinds of outcomes the system produces in areas like health,
        transportation, education, and media.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.B.2</title>
      <p>
        AI impacts can change when a tool is deployed in a new context or used beyond its
        original purpose. In these cases, mismatches between training data, design assumptions,
        and real-world use can lead to different outcomes.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.B.3</title>
      <p>
        The scale of an AI system refers to how widely it is used and how much it influences
        outcomes, such as the number of users, the volume of decisions it helps make, how often
        it is used, and the number of contexts in which it is used, including different schools,
        jobs, regions, or languages.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.B.4</title>
      <p>
        When an AI tool is used on a large scale, even small design choices, errors, or biased
        patterns can affect many people, and impacts that seem minor for one user can become
        significant across a large population. AI tools can also influence individual behavior
        and habits. When many people rely on the same tool, those shifts can accumulate and
        reshape group norms and expectations, such as how people write, search, or communicate,
        even if the tool was originally intended as only a helper.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.B.5</title>
      <p>
        AI can produce uneven outcomes through multiple pathways. These may include training data
        and labels that shape which users the system works well for, design choices that determine
        what errors are tolerated and what feedback users receive, and decision-making practices
        that determine how outputs are applied in real life.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.B.6</title>
      <p>
        AI can reshape work and economic systems by changing the cost, speed, and quality of tasks.
        This can increase productivity and create new jobs and skills needs, while also contributing
        to job displacement or changes in wages and working conditions.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.B.7</title>
      <p>
        AI shapes culture and communication by filtering what people see and what content gets
        created and shared. When these systems are widely used, they can influence what spreads,
        what is rewarded with attention, and what people consider normal or trustworthy.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.B.8</title>
      <p>
        AI systems can have environmental impacts due to energy use, computing infrastructure,
        and material consumption.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.6.B.9</title>
      <p>
        To analyze AI's broader impact, consider how the system is used: identify who uses it and
        who is affected, where it is deployed and at what scale, and how outputs influence decisions
        and behavior. Use these observations to determine who benefits, who may be harmed or left
        out, and what changes when context or scale changes.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="LOs-4-6">
    <title>Learning Objectives Review</title>

    <remark>
      <title>4.6.A</title>
      <p>
        Evaluate the implementation of AI systems using ethical design criteria.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>4.6.B</title>
      <p>
        Explain how AI impacts society, the economy, and culture.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="vocab-4-6">
   <title>Vocabulary Review</title>
  </subsection>
</section>
